# Line endings, settled once.
#
# Git was warning on every commit ("LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time
# Git touches it") because core.autocrlf=true was left to guess, while the
# index is already 100% LF and the working tree had drifted almost entirely to
# LF as well (130 of 131 tracked text files). The warning was noise about a
# conversion nobody wanted.
#
# `eol=lf` is what stops it: it pins the *checkout* to LF, so there is no
# CRLF round-trip left to warn about. It is safe here precisely because the
# index is already LF -- `text=auto` normalises the working tree the same way
# autocrlf already did, so nothing is renormalised and no file changes content.
# MSVC, CMake and PowerShell all read LF without complaint.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The exception, for when one turns up: a batch file with LF endings can be
# mis-parsed by cmd.exe mid-script. Nothing tracked matches this today; the
# rule is here so that adding one does not quietly break.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# ---- Binary: never converted, never diffed as text --------------------------
# Marked by *type*, not by directory. `assets/` is mostly text -- the avatar
# sprite sheets under assets/avatars are commented ASCII art whose columns are
# the drawing, and assets/prompts holds Markdown -- so a blanket `assets/** -text`
# would hide the diffs most worth reading. Likewise third_party/ is vendored
# *source* (json.hpp, miniaudio.h). So the binary list is by extension, which
# covers those directories and everywhere else at the same time.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.bmp  binary
*.ico  binary
*.psd  binary
*.webp binary
*.mp3  binary
*.wav  binary
*.ogg  binary
*.flac binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip  binary
*.7z   binary
*.gz   binary
*.bz2  binary
*.tar  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.exe  binary
*.dll  binary
*.lib  binary
*.pdb  binary
*.obj  binary
*.spv  binary

# Model weights and the engines' data files. models/ is gitignored today, but
# these turn up under spikes/ and would be catastrophic to line-ending-convert.
*.onnx binary
*.gguf binary
*.vvm  binary
*.bin  binary
*.fst  binary
*.dic  binary
*.safetensors binary

# ---- Diff hints ------------------------------------------------------------
*.h   diff=cpp
*.hpp diff=cpp
*.cpp diff=cpp
*.md  diff=markdown
